 To find the area of triangle $OAB$, we first need to find the vectors $\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\overrightarrow{OB}$. The vectors are already given as $\overrightarrow{OA} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\overrightarrow{OB} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. The vector $\overrightarrow{AB}$ can be found as $\overrightarrow{AB} = \overrightarrow{OB} - \overrightarrow{OA} = \begin{pmatrix} -3-1 \\ -2-2 \\ 1-3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ -4 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$. The cross product of $\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\overrightarrow{AB}$ will give us a vector perpendicular to both $\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\overrightarrow{AB}$, and its magnitude will be twice the area of the triangle. The cross product is calculated as follows: \[
\overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{AB} = \begin{pmatrix} i & j & k \\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\ -4 & -4 & -2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} (2 \cdot -2) - (3 \cdot -4) \\ (3 \cdot -4) - (1 \cdot -2) \\ (1 \cdot -4) - (2 \cdot -4) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -4 + 12 \\ -12 + 2 \\ -4 + 8 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ -10 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}
\] The magnitude of this cross product vector is \[
\| \overrightarrow{OA} \times \overrightarrow{AB} \| = \sqrt{8^2 + (-10)^2 + 4^2} = \sqrt{64 + 100 + 16} = \sqrt{180} = 6\sqrt{5}
\] The area of triangle $OAB$ is half of this magnitude, so \[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 6\sqrt{5} = 3\sqrt{5}
\] So the area of triangle $OAB$ is $\boxed{3\sqrt{5}}$ </think> <answer> $3\sqrt{5}$ </answer>
